<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Celebrations and Traditions</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> I Celebrate This Tradition Because...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Present on a personally significant topic. Interactive: Engage in discussions about various real-world topics, asking and responding to questions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can present about a personally significant tradition, directly addressing the specific standard (e.g., "My family celebrates Christmas with special food."). Can engage in discussions about cultural traditions by asking and responding to questions and using follow-up prompts (e.g., "What is your favorite tradition?" "I like Fiestas Patrias because of the parades." "What do you like most about the parade?"). Can ask and respond to questions about regional festivals by providing relevant details (e.g., "When do people celebrate the Manito Ocuero Festival?" "We celebrate it in August.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; text-align: left; width: 40%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; text-align: left; width: 40%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; text-align: left; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows 3-4 vivid images of different Panamanian celebrations (e.g., Fiestas Patrias parade, a traditional dance, Christmas family gathering). Students are asked to identify each celebration and share one word or phrase they associate with it in pairs, then share with the class. Example prompt: "What celebration is this? What comes to your mind when you see it?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models describing one of the traditions using target grammar and vocabulary, paying attention to pronunciation. "This is Fiestas Patrias. Panama celebrates its independence on November 3rd. People typically march in parades. I am excited about watching the folk dances." The teacher explicitly models word stress on "celebration," "cultural," and the /s/, /z/, /ɪz/ sounds in verbs like "decorates" /s/, "gathers" /z/, "wishes" /ɪz/ when applicable.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key vocabulary and concepts.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a 'parade' a type of food or a public procession?" (Expected answer: A public procession.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we 'celebrate' something sad or something happy?" (Expected answer: Something happy.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Celebrations and Traditions")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio dialogue (or teacher-read dialogue) between two Panamanian teenagers discussing their favorite family traditions for a school project. It focuses on using Present Simple, Past Simple, Gerunds after prepositions, and target vocabulary.
                    <br><i>Dialogue Excerpt:</i>
                    <br><i>Ana: "Hi Miguel! What's your favorite tradition?"</i>
                    <br><i>Miguel: "Definitely Carnaval! My family celebrates it every year. We usually travel to Las Tablas. People have participated in many parades and traditional dances for centuries there. I love watching the floats and the water games. I am excited about gathering with my cousins and enjoying the festive atmosphere."</i>
                    <br><i>Ana: "Wow! That sounds fun. My favorite tradition is Christmas. My family decorates our home together. We traditionally prepare a big dinner. My grandmother always says, 'We enjoy celebrating our heritage together.' Christmas reminds me of Panama becoming independent in 1903, because it's a time for family unity."</i>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Engage in class discussions about regional and cultural celebrations, responding to questions about traditions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the dialogue and answer gist questions, orally:
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is Miguel's favorite tradition?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do people traditionally do during Carnaval according to Miguel?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why does Ana enjoy Christmas?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice using Present Simple, Past Simple, Gerunds after prepositions, and Present Perfect to describe traditions and express preferences, focusing on correct pronunciation of word stress and third-person singular verb endings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Builder:</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides sentence beginnings with gaps focusing on grammar points. Students complete them orally using target vocabulary. E.g., "I am excited about ____ (celebrate) Christmas with my family." (Answer: celebrating). "My community _____ (gather) for the ____ (parade) every year." (Answer: gathers, parade). Teacher monitors for correct verb forms and pronunciation, especially for '-s' endings like "celebrates" /s/, "gathers" /z/, "watches" /ɪz/.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Guided Interview Questions:</b> Students receive a list of 4-5 guided questions (e.g., "What tradition do people celebrate in your community?", "When do they usually observe this custom?", "What do you like most about this festival?"). In pairs, students take turns asking and answering these questions, using the provided sentence structures and vocabulary. They are encouraged to use sequencers (first, next, finally) and conditionals (if, when). E.g., "When do people celebrate the Manito Ocuero Festival?" "We celebrate it in August."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Preference Sharing:</b> Students are given prompts like "My favorite tradition is ____ because ____." or "I enjoy _____ (verb+ing) because ____." They engage in short conversations with a new partner, expressing their preferences about different celebrations. They also ask follow-up questions like "What do you like most about the parade?" Peer check: Partners listen and provide feedback on clear pronunciation and use of at least one new vocabulary word.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-20 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Celebrations and Traditions")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "My Favorite Tradition Mini-Presentation". Students will individually present for 1-1.5 minutes about a personally significant tradition to a small group (3-4 students). They will describe the tradition, explain what people traditionally do, and most importantly, why it is important to them, directly addressing "I Celebrate This Tradition Because...".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student selects one personally significant tradition (e.g., Christmas, Fiestas Patrias, a family custom).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They quickly jot down 3-4 keywords or phrases using target vocabulary (e.g., "gather," "family," "celebrate independence," "special cuisine," "parade," "heritage," "festive atmosphere").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In small groups, students take turns presenting their tradition. They must include:
                            <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What tradition they celebrate.</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">When it's celebrated (if, when).</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What people traditionally do (e.g., "They decorate their homes with the national flag and participate in the parades.").</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Why it is important or special to them (e.g., "I celebrate this tradition because it unites my family and reminds us of our ancestry.").</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After each presentation, group members ask at least one follow-up question (e.g., "What is your favorite part about that celebration?", "Have you ever participated in a traditional dance during that festival?").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their "My Favorite Tradition Mini-Presentation" to their small group, demonstrating their ability to describe a tradition and explain its significance, and interact with questions from peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist / Peer Feedback):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear word stress (e.g., "celebration," "cultural") and correct pronunciation of third-person singular verbs (-s, -es, -ies sounds).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> (Used at least 5-7 words from the unit, e.g., tradition, parade, celebrate, gather, heritage).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target grammar:</b> (Correctly used Present Simple, Past Simple, Gerunds after prepositions, and Present Perfect when describing experiences).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and coherence:</b> Clearly described the tradition and explained why it is personally significant.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Engagement in discussions:</b> Asked and responded to questions effectively.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick written reflection.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was one new word or phrase you used today to talk about traditions?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was challenging about speaking about your tradition, and what was easy?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How confident do you feel describing different traditions to others now?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific parts of speaking about celebrations (vocabulary, grammar, pronunciation) do you want to practice more?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in promoting student speaking and interaction today? (e.g., specific activities, grouping strategies).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with fluency, grammar, or vocabulary during the speaking tasks? (e.g., more sentence frames, focused pronunciation drills).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can the ideas and oral practice from this lesson connect to and facilitate the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition) where students will write about traditions?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition), students will use the ideas, vocabulary, and sentence structures they practiced orally today to write descriptive paragraphs about their favorite traditions, explaining "I Celebrate This Tradition Because...".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>